{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 36, :comments ["<b>And the omer is a tenth of an ephah.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said: an ephah is three se'ah; a se'ah is six qavim; a qab is four log; a log is six eggs. And the mnemonic is גוד״ו אילנא. It turns out that the qab is 24 eggs, the se'ah is 144 eggs, the ephah is 432 eggs. 43 eggs and a fifth of an egg is the measure of dough that becomes liable in ḥallah, and it is the measure for kneading on Passover. And the question arises as to why until now he did not explain the measure of the omer, for it would have been fitting to mention it earlier, either at the command of HaShem, blessed be He, saying 'an omer per head is a tenth of an ephah,' or at the event, saying 'and they measured by the omer, which is a tenth of an ephah.' But the reason is that the entire passage expounds the matter of the manna, and once the account of the manna's affairs was completed, he needed to explain now at the end what the measure was. Or one may say: it was so that the measure would be placed adjacent to the matter of Refidim, for it is the way of the world that amid abundance and plenitude of blessing a person grows slack from divine service and weakens in Torah, whereas under rationing and a moderate measure he would not do so, but would strengthen himself in it. Therefore Scripture informs us that Israel, amid the rationing and the moderate measure for each and every one — which is a tenth of an ephah — loosened their hands at Refidim from Torah; therefore 'and Amaleq came' (Exodus 17:8). And this is the juxtaposition of these passages one to the other. And as for why the measure of the omer was one out of ten, it is possible to say that its reason is like the reason of the commandment of the tithe (ma'aser), for Israel in the wilderness were the holy ones of the Most High, embodied angels; and when they ate this measure of that supernal manna, fine and pure, their thought would roam in the tenth measure and they would contemplate its significance, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 24:11): ״ויחזו את האלהים ויאכלו וישתו״, and through this they were prepared to receive intelligibles and worthy to attain supernal attainments at the holy assembly at Mount Sinai, where 'they shall go, day and night they shall go and not grow weary' (cf. Isaiah 40:31 — 'they shall run and not grow weary')."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "The mnemonic גוד״ו אילנא (a mnemonic tree) encodes the ratio chain 3-6-4-6 by initial letters; the ephah total of תל״ב = 432 eggs is given as printed (3×6×4×6=432). The final phrase 'ילכו ולא ייעפו' echoes Isaiah 40:31; no citation is given in the source; the hyper-literal rendering preserves the fused allusion."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:00:22.922741046Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}